{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"`scripts/backtest_bugfix_size.py --check-planning-corpus` exits 1 on current `origin/main`:\n\n```\nplanning-corpus check: corpus=114 (want 113); missed=[]; mechanical_fp=[]; FAIL\n```\n\nReproduced UNCHANGED on a pristine detached worktree at `origin/main` (with the tracker symlinked in), so this is not caused by ticket 1dd5's change: the flagged set is still exactly `ADJUDICATED_SUBSTANTIVE` (`missed=[]`, `mechanical_fp=[]`); only the corpus SIZE arm fails.\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nRoot cause shape: the corpus is described in the module docstring as 'pinned and reproducible', but it is re-derived at run time from LIVE ticket state — the ancestors of `PLANNING_CORPUS_BOUNDARY` whose subject prefix resolves to a ticket whose CURRENT `ticket_type` is `bug` (`_resolve_bug_ticket` -> `_ticket_type`). Reclassifying any historical ticket to/from `bug`, or adding a ticket that changes short-prefix resolution, moves the count. `PLANNING_CORPUS_SIZE = 113` is therefore a constant asserted against a moving derivation.\n\nNothing in CI or the test suite runs this script, so the drift went unnoticed.\n\n## Approach\n\nPin the corpus by IDENTITY, not count: replace `PLANNING_CORPUS_SIZE = 113` with a `PLANNING_CORPUS_COMMITS` frozenset of the exact 113 commit SHAs the planning analysis adjudicated (recovered by re-running the derivation in `scripts/backtest_bugfix_size.py` `main()` against the tracker's historical state at the pin commit 1920dc9fe559 / tickets branch 94ec395ee9b2, via the `REBAR_TRACKER_DIR` override in `src/rebar/config.py:tracker_dir`). In `--check-planning-corpus` mode, iterate those pinned SHAs directly (measuring each with the existing `_measure`) instead of re-deriving membership from live ticket state; assert the measured set's identity equals the pin and the flagged set equals `ADJUDICATED_SUBSTANTIVE` exactly. Per-row ticket labels stay best-effort resolved for the artifact only — they no longer gate. This fits the problem because the adjudication was a one-time, hand-reviewed judgment over a fixed commit set; identity pinning is the only assertion shape immune to tracker churn. Align the module docstring so 'pinned and reproducible' matches what the code actually does.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `scripts/backtest_bugfix_size.py` — pin the 113 SHAs, derive check-mode rows from the pin, align the docstring.\n\n## Testing\n\nMechanical constant + control-flow change to a standalone analysis script that nothing in CI or the test suite imports; the executable proof is the script's own check mode: `python scripts/backtest_bugfix_size.py --check-planning-corpus` exits 0 (happy path), and the check still fails (exit 1) if the flagged set diverges from `ADJUDICATED_SUBSTANTIVE` — verified by inspection of the retained assertion arm.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `python scripts/backtest_bugfix_size.py --check-planning-corpus` exits 0 on `origin/main`, OR the size assertion is replaced by one that does not depend on live ticket state (e.g. pin the 113 SHAs, not the count). Checked by running the command in the worktree and observing exit 0.\n- [ ] The decision is recorded in the script's docstring so the 'pinned and reproducible' claim matches what the code does. Checked by reading the updated docstring."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787432119914893001,"uuid":"d17e9a44-8bd3-4716-b0ea-8fc270c499e8"}